# A Novel Conductive Mesoporous Layer with a Dynamic Two-Step Deposition Strategy Boosts Efficiency of Perovskite Solar Cells to 20%

## abstract
Lead halide perovskite solar cells (PSCs) with the high power conversion efficiency (PCE) typically use mesoporous metal oxide nanoparticles as the scaffold and electron-transport layers. However, the traditional mesoporous layer suffers from low electron conductivity and severe carrier recombination. Here, antimony-doped tin oxide nanorod arrays are proposed as novel transparent conductive mesoporous layers in PSCs. Such a mesoporous layer improves the electron transport as well as light utilization. To resolve the common problem of uneven growth of perovskite on rough surface, the dynamic two-step spin coating strategy is proposed to prepare highly smooth, dense, and crystallized perovskite films with micrometer-scale grains, largely reducing the carrier recombination ratio. The conductive mesoporous layer and high-quality perovskite film eventually render the PSC with a remarkable PCE of 20.1% with excellent reproducibility. These findings provide a new avenue to further design high-efficiency PSCs from the aspect of carrier transport and recombination.

Lead halide perovskite solar cells (PSCs) with the high power conversion efficiency (PCE) typically use mesoporous metal oxide nanoparticles as the scaffold and electron-transport layers. However, the traditional mesoporous layer suffers from low electron conductivity and severe carrier recombination. Here, antimony-doped tin oxide nanorod arrays are proposed as novel transparent conductive mesoporous layers in PSCs. Such a mesoporous layer improves the electron transport as well as light utilization. To resolve the common problem of uneven growth of perovskite on rough surface, the dynamic two-step spin coating strategy is proposed to prepare highly smooth, dense, and crystallized perovskite films with micrometer-scale grains, largely reducing the carrier recombination ratio. The conductive mesoporous layer and high-quality perovskite film eventually render the PSC with a remarkable PCE of $20.1\%$ with excellent reproducibility. These findings provide a new avenue to further design high-efficiency PSCs from the aspect of carrier transport and recombination.
Hybrid organic–inorganic perovskites have become as promising light-sensitive materials and widely used in light-emitting diodes,[1–3] photodetectors,[4–6] and solar cells, $[7-9]$ owing to their broad spectral absorption, high carrier mobility, small exciton binding energy, and good solution processability.[10–12] Since the first report in 2009,[13] the perovskite solar cells (PSCs) have made impressive strides with the maximum power conversion efficiency (PCE) increasing from $3.8\%$ to over $22.1\%$ .[13–21] PSCs can be fabricated with a planar or mesoporous structure.[22,23] Although planar structure PSCs have advantages of the low cost and easy processing, the PCE of planar solar cells is still lower than that of mesoporous structure. The current record PSCs reported by Sang Il Seok adopt an architecture of fluorine-doped tin oxide (FTO)/compact $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ /mesoporous $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and perovskite composite layer/ perovskite upper layer/PTAA/Au, which is classified as the mesoporous type.[20] As for mesoporous PSCs, a mesoporous metal oxide nanoparticle layer serves as the scaffold to support perovskite and electron transporting layer to extract and transfer photocarriers injected from the perovskite.[24–26] Compared with a planartype device, mesoporous architecture is more tolerant toward the number of defects in perovskite and ion vacancy migration, which are regarded as the main causes of hysteresis. However, some vital issues still remain, such as poor electron transport and severe charge recombination caused by the low electron mobility of metal oxides, numerous grain boundaries, and surface defects. Despite various attempts have been made, including element doping and interface engineering,[27–30] the aforementioned problems are still inevitable. An alternative effective method is developing conductive mesoporous layers which can simultaneously maintain the high surface area and fast electron transportation. Recently, Kim and co-workers used an electrochemical etching way to modify the surface of FTO into a porous conductive structure and obtained a remarkable PCE of $19.22\%$ , demonstrating the great potential of conductive mesoporous layers.[31] It is well known that 1D semiconductor $\ensuremath{\left(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\right.}$ and $\mathrm{{znO}}$ , etc.) nanostructure arrays possess direct electron pathways and excellent light harvesting capability and thus have been used as mesoporous layers to enhance carrier transfer in PSCs.[32–37] Therefore, we can anticipate that significantly improved performance can be realized when 1D conductive arrays are utilized as mesoporous layers, which have not been explored.
Antimony-doped tin oxide (ATO) has been widely used as a photoelectrode of solar cell due to its several features of high light transmittance, good electrical conductivity, low cost, and environmental stability.[38–40] Herein, for the first time, we propose
1D ATO nanorod arrays as novel conductive mesoporous layers of PSCs. Well-aligned ATO nanorod arrays on FTO substrate (ATO–FTO) with up to $90\%$ transmittance and down to $39.8\Omega/\mathrm{sq}$ resistivity are obtained. Through developing a dynamic two-step spin coating method based on the combined principle of onestep antisolvent and traditional two-step spin coating, a uniformly dense perovskite film with grain size up to micrometer scale is successfully prepared. With the conductive ATO mesoporous layer and dynamic two-step deposition strategy, the champion PCE of PSCs reaches $20.1\%$ . The control device without conductive ATO mesoporous layer can also deliver a PCE of $17.8\%$ , showing the great adaptability of our new preparation method.
In order to obtain 1D conductive mesoporous layers with low resistance and high transmittance, $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ nanorod arrays with various Sb doping ratios have been investigated. With the increased concentration of Sb precursor, the amount of Sb in the final $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ is controlled from $4.7\%$ to $10.3\%$ , which is calibrated by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy shown in Figure S1 in the Supporting Information. The binding energy range of $529{-}543~\mathrm{eV}$ is related to O 1s and Sb 3d transition.
The Gaussian fitted line of $\mathrm{Sb}\ 3\mathrm{d}3/2$ is centered at $540.1\ \mathrm{eV},$ which is characteristic of $\mathrm{Sb^{5+}}$ .[41] This indicates that the conductivity of ATO originates from the substitution of $\mathrm{Sn^{4+}}$ by $\mathrm{Sb^{5+}}$ .[42] As the doping ratio increases to $7.8\%$ , the ATO–FTO square resistance rapidly decreases to $39.8~\Omega/\mathrm{sq}$ and almost remains unchanged with a higher doping ratio (Figure 1a). The huge resistance reduction can be explained by the lifted Fermi level and narrowed bandgap of ATO. The ATO Fermi level position is calculated from ultraviolet photoelectron spectroscopy spectra (Figure S2a, Supporting Information), and the enlarged views around the secondary electron cutoff position $(E_{\mathrm{Cutoff}})$ (Figure S2b, Supporting Information) and the instrument Fermi energy position $(E_{\mathrm{Fermi}})$ (Figure S2c, Supporting Information). We can clearly find that $E_{\mathrm{Cutoff}}$ increases gradually with doping ratio, which means the decrease of work function and the corresponding increase of Fermi level. Moreover, the UV– vis absorption spectra (Figure 1b) show a tiny red shift, suggesting that the bandgap narrows as doping. Both the increased Fermi level and narrowed bandgap result in the transform of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ from semiconductor and conductor.
As shown in Figure S3 in the Supporting Information, the doping ratio has an obvious influence on the morphology. With the increased doping ratio, the nanorod diameter increases gradually and eventually fills up the space between nanorods,[43,44] which will affect the light transmittance. Figure 1c shows that all the ATO nanorod arrays with various Sb doping ratios have high transmittance accompanied by strong Fresnel interference. To identify the best light transmittance, we integrate the transmission spectra with the AM $1.5{\mathrm{~G~}}$ solar spectrum. The maximum theoretical current density by assuming $100\%$ internal quantum efficiency corresponds to ATO nanorod array with the doping ratio of $7.8\%$ (Figure 1d). Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images of $7.8\%$ doped ATO nanorod arrays are shown in Figure 1e,f. Vertically aligned ATO nanorod arrays with an average diameter of $70\ \mathrm{nm}$ and a uniform thickness around $310~\mathrm{nm}$ are evenly distributed on the FTO substrate. The X-ray diffraction (XRD) pattern of samples is shown in Figure S4 in the Supporting Information. Three strong peaks appear at
$26.5^{\circ}$ , $33.9^{\circ}$ , and $37.9^{\circ}$ corresponding to the diffraction of tetragonal $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ (110), (101), and (200) crystal plane, and no impurity peaks are found, indicating that Sb doping does not affect the phase of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ . In short, the designed ATO nanorod array has a 1D porous morphology, a low square resistance down to $39.8~\Omega/\mathrm{sq}$ , and a high transmittance up to $90\%$ , which fully meets the requirement of a conductive mesoporous layer.
Based on the conductive ATO mesoporous substrate, $6~\mathrm{nm}$ thick $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ is deposited through atomic layer deposition (ALD) as electronic transport layer (ETL) followed by preparing the perovskite films. To obtain high-quality perovskite films, conventional one-step antisolvent and two-step sequential deposition methods are applied. Unfortunately, both of these methods cannot fabricate highly dense, pinhole free, and large grainsized films. As for the one-step antisolvent method, the perov­ skite film has many pinholes in spite of the relatively large grain size induced by the fast crystallization (Figure 2a,b). The pinholes would provide a direct path for ETL and hole transport layer and cause a huge leakage current. The highest PCE of PSCs based on one-step method is only $7.97\%$ (Figure 3a). As for two-step spin coating, the ATO nanorods are fully covered by the perovskite film without pinholes. However, the maximum grain size is less than $500~\mathrm{nm}$ , and a large number of grain boundaries exist in the perovskite film (Figure 2c,d), which will impede the charge transport and increase the carrier recombination. The corresponding $J{-}V$ curve indicates a PCE of $14.18\%$ . In view of the above observation, to achieve a perovskite film with full coverage and large grain size, a possible solution is introducing antisolvent theory to the two-step spin coating procedure. In brief, the N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF) solution of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ is spin coated on the compact $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/$ ATO substrate at 3000 rpm for $10\mathrm{~s~}$ and the isopropyl alcohol solution of methylammonium iodide (MAI) is immediately dropwise added while keeping the whole spinning time 20 s prior to the end of spin coating. The yellow $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ solution turns to brown immediately and gradually darkens during the spin coating. After annealing at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $10~\mathrm{min}$ , the as-prepared perovskite film has a shiny black color due to the high visible light absorption and super flat surface. The top-view SEM images of perovskite film are shown in Figure 2e,f. The perovskite film exhibits micrometer-scale-sized grains without pinholes. The cross-sectional view SEM image of PSCs (Figure S5, Supporting Information) shows that the perovskite film has the flattest interface. The corresponding PCE is measured to be $17.71\%$ . All the photovoltaic parameters of PSCs with different perovskite preparation methods are summarized in Table S1 in the Supporting Information.
In order to investigate the charge transport within the PSCs, the electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) was measured as shown in Figure 3b. All the curves contain two circular arcs. The charge transport resistance $(R_{\mathrm{ct}})$ at the ETL/perovskite interface can be fitted from the high-frequency arc, while the lowerfrequency arc represents the charge recombination resistance $\left(R_{\mathrm{rec}}\right)$ , which is inversely proportional to recombination rate.[45–47] The fitted values of series resistance $\left(R_{\mathrm{s}}\right)$ , $R_{\mathrm{rec}},$ and $R_{\mathrm{{ct}}}$ are listed in Table S2 in the Supporting Information. As for PSCs based on one-step method, the $R_{\mathrm{rec}}$ is $269~\Omega$ which is comparable to the $R_{\mathrm{ct}}$ $(238~\Omega)$ . The existence of many pinholes is responsible for the quite low $R_{\mathrm{rec}}$ . As for traditional two-step prepared PSCs, the full coverage of perovskite film without pinholes reduces the $R_{\mathrm{{ct}}}$ to $86.2~\Omega$ , but the $R_{\mathrm{rec}}$ is still low $(1.01\ \mathrm{k}\Omega)$ owing to the large number of grain boundaries. For the device with our new preparation method, the $R_{\mathrm{{ct}}}$ keeps almost the same while the $R_{\mathrm{rec}}$ can double to $2.23\ \mathrm{k}\Omega$ . The increased $R_{\mathrm{rec}}$ is mainly ascribed to the improved perovskite morphology with a smooth surface, full coverage, and large grain size. We also check the crystallization of perovskite films with different preparation methods and the XRD patterns are displayed in Figure 3c. Except for the signals from the perovskite phase, diffraction peaks of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ cannot be observed. The diffraction peak intensity of traditional two-step prepared perovskite film is low and the full width at half maximum (FWHM) of (110) crystal face is $0.26^{\circ}$ . In comparison, the dynamic two-step method renders the perovskite film with increased diffraction intensity and an FWHM as low as $0.16^{\circ}$ , suggesting the largely improved crystallization.
The compact $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ plays an important role in determining the PCE since it serves dual function as ETL and hole blocking layer. ALD processed $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ with different thicknesses (2, 6, 10, and $15\ \mathrm{nm}$ ) are employed to investigate its effect on device performance. The typical transmission electron microscopy in Figure S6 in the Supporting Information shows that an ATO nanorod of approximately $90~\mathrm{nm}$ diameter is evenly coated by a $10\ \mathrm{nm}$ thick $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ shell. The corresponding $J{-}V$ curves of
PSCs are shown in Figure 4a and the photovoltaic parameters are listed in Table S3 in the Supporting Information. Using a $2\ \mathrm{nm}$ thick $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , the performance is rather poor with a low $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ and $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ . As the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ thickness increases, the PCE gradually increases and $10\mathrm{nm}$ thick $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ enables the best PCE of $20.1\%$ , with a $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of $1.10\mathrm{V}_{:}$ , $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of $23.81\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , and FF of $76.75\%$ . A higher $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ thickness leads to a slightly reduced PCE. The EIS of devices with different thicknesses of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ is measured and the Nyquist plot is shown in Figure 4b, and the fitted $R_{s},$ $R_{\mathrm{ct}},$ and $R_{\mathrm{rec}}$ are listed in Table S4 in the Supporting Information. We can observe that both $R_{\mathrm{{ct}}}$ and $R_{\mathrm{rec}}$ increase as the thickness of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ increases. The existence of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ prevents the recombination of holes in the perovskite with the electrons in the conductive ATO layer, which is in agreement with the trend of increased $R_{\mathrm{rec}}$ as increasing $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ thickness. In particular, the $R_{\mathrm{{ct}}}$ $(123\ \Omega)$ of 10 nm $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ -based device is slightly higher than that $(94.9~\Omega)$ of $6\ \mathrm{nm}\ \mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ -based device, while the $R_{\mathrm{rec}}$ can reach $8.62\ \mathrm{k}\Omega$ , which is much higher than $1.01\ \mathrm{k}\Omega$ . The increased $R_{\mathrm{rec}}$ means a reduced carrier recombination. The impressive PCE of $20.1\%$ suggests that $10\ \mathrm{nm}$ thick $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ is sufficient to suppress the carrier recombination. With the thickness further increasing to $15\mathrm{nm}$ , the $R_{\mathrm{rec}}$ slightly decreases since additional $5\mathrm{nm}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ may introduce more recombination in the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer itself. We also prepared PSCs by using undoped $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ nanorod array with $10\mathrm{nm}$ thick $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ . The corresponding $J{-}V$ curve and EIS results are shown in Figure 4c,d, respectively. The fitted $R_{\mathrm{s}}$ , $R_{\mathrm{{ct}}},$ and $R_{\mathrm{rec}}$ are listed in Table S5 in the Supporting Information. The undoped $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ -based PSC shows a poor PCE of only $12.16\%$ with a high $R_{\mathrm{{ct}}}$ and significantly reduced $R_{\mathrm{rec}}$ .
The $J{-}V$ curves of the champion device are measured at a reverse and forward scan in order to investigate the hysteresis effect of PSCs, as shown in Figure 5a, indicating a hysteresis index of $9\%$ . Coincidentally, the same hysteresis index is obtained for the planar type of PSCs without a conductive ATO mesoporous layer (Figure 5b), which implies that the hysteresis effect is mainly induced by the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL. To better understand the optoelectric property, we measured the external quantum efficiency (EQE) of the champion and the planar type of PSCs (Figure 5c). As for the mesoporous PSCs, the EQE value is above $80\%$ in the wavelength range of $400{\mathrm{-}}700\ \mathrm{nm}$ and the maximum EQE can reach $90\%$ under $500\ \mathrm{nm}$ light illumination. This value is very close to the maximum light transmittance of the conductive ATO layer, which means the nearly $100\%$ photon utilization in the perovskite film. Compared with planar PSCs, the EQE value in the short wavelength range is almost the same while it is much higher in the long wavelength range beyond $550\mathrm{nm}$ . This can be explained by the pronounced light scattering in the mesoporous ATO nanorod array and thus the increased light absorption in the perovskite layer, which is proved by UV–vis absorption spectra (Figure S7, Supporting Information). The integrated current density of mesoporous and planar PSCs is calculated to be 21.18 and $20.06\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , respectively. The commonly observed lower current density from EQE compared with $J{-}V$ curves is related to unavoidable surface trap states of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL.[48,49] The EIS measurement of two different structures is shown in Figure 5d and the fitted parameters are listed in Table S6 in the Supporting Information. As for the mesoporous PSCs, the $R_{\mathrm{{ct}}}$ is obviously lower than that of the planar type device. We prepare a batch of 30 devices based on the two structures and the PCE distribution is exhibited in Figure 5e,f. An average PCE of $19.04\%$ and $17.09\%$ is obtained for the mesoporous and planar PSCs, respectively, suggesting that the present devices have a good reproducibility. It is noted that the champion device employs the ATO nanorods array with a thickness of around $310\ \mathrm{nm}$ . The thickness of the ATO nanorods can be easily tuned by the hydrothermal reaction time. The side-view SEM images of the ATO nanorods and corresponding $J{-}V$ curved are presented in Figure S8 in the Supporting Information. Holding the device at the maximum power point for 500 s under continuous illumination, it maintains a stable output and only a $1.8\%$ decrease is observed (Figure S9a, Supporting Information). The $J{-}V$ curves at the reverse scan are also recorded with a fixed voltage step and different delay time from 30 to $400~\mathrm{{ms}}$ (Figure S9b, Supporting Information).
The slight decrease of PCE demonstrates the device has a weak scan rate dependence.
In summary, a novel conductive ATO mesoporous layer and dynamic two-step perovskite preparation method is successfully applied in PSCs to obtain high conversion efficiency. The 1D conductive ATO mesoporous layer shows the light transmittance up to $90\%$ and square resistance down to $39.8~\Omega/\mathrm{sq}$ . The dynamic two-step method produces perovskite films with micrometer-scale-sized grains and ultrasmooth surface and is adaptable in both mesoporous and planar structures. The mesoporous PSCs achieve a remarkable PCE of $20.1\%$ with up to $90\%$ EQE and almost $100\%$ photon utilization in the visible wavelength range, meanwhile, the planar device can also reach a PCE of $17.8\%$ using the present preparation method of perovskite films. These results highlight the advance of both conductive mesoporous layer and dynamic two-step methods for highly efficient PSCs in the future.
